of cancer organizations and  toxicologists  and dermatologists without much evidence  and citing a beauty blogger  it's really just a lot in the one paper  well i think looking at what else the  authors have done gives a bit of insight  here  like i said four of them are from an  ocean research institute  and one of them used to be a human  toxicologist i think the obvious  question is  why didn't they collaborate with an  academic who currently works in human  toxicology the only author who  specializes in human toxicology on this  paper that talks a lot about human  toxicology  is someone who firstly isn't affiliated  with an academic institution  but also he's retired and he doesn't  have a phd  it's weird i know this sounds elitist  but that's just how scientific academia  works  your first step in your training is  getting a phd and it's not a one-off  either  it's a bit of a pattern in the  publication history of the first two  authors  craig downes and joseph donato craig  downs is the scientist who published and  promoted the choral studies that led to  the whole reefsafe sunscreen thing in  2018. he's been very active in getting  particular chemical sunscreens banned in  places like hawaii  since then he's published more studies  on how sunscreen impacts aquatic  organisms  which is what you'd expect from a marine  biologist but he's also started  collaborating with joseph donato author  2  on papers about human toxicology the  only link between these papers  and his older work is that they're all  about how bad chemical sunscreen is  in all sorts of different ways which is  weird scientists usually have expertise  in a specific field  and they specialize in particular  methodologies they don't jump around  different fields focusing on a specific  group of chemicals  this publication history doesn't make a  lot of sense in my opinion  but if you look at some of the other  things that downs and dinardo have said  outside of their peer review  publications then  you can sort of see that all roads seem  to lead back to chemical sunscreens  increasing melanoma rates it's because  of too much sunscreen even though  dermatologists and the american academy  of dermatology  and the skin cancer foundation all say  that sunscreen can reduce skin cancer  they say that the increasing melanoma  rates are because of better detection  and different lifestyle habits  apparently they're wrong declining coral  reefs um it's my professional opinion  that most of the coral reefs in the last  40 years have died because your bathroom  than anything else climate change oil  spills what have you  i think this goes without saying but  this is very much not what most coral  scientists think  the consensus is that the biggest  threats to coral are climate change  ecosystem changes from things like  overfishing and land-based pollution  from things like agriculture  there's a recent open access review that  looks at all the studies on sunscreen  and coral  the measurements that downs took around  hawaii were ten to a thousand times  higher than any other published  measurements  the conclusion is organic uv filters do  occur in the environment  but according to our analysis there is  limited evidence to suggest  that their presence is causing  significant harm to coral reefs  although more research should be done as  you might have expected  donato has said that their lead author  was hired by big sunscreen  now i think there are definitely lots of  legitimate criticisms of the beauty and  personal care industry  and we can't just trust everything they  say i mean i talk about marketing myths  all the time but that doesn't mean that  the complete opposite of what the  industry says  is always going to be true and what are  the chances that everyone in the  industry and in toxicology dermatology  and coral science are all in on the same  conspiracy to protect big sunscreen  people like to blab in a decades-old  conspiracy there's going to be thousands  of people involved  how has no one leaked receipts about all  their secret meetings and how people are  getting paid off  i think if you have to dispute the  scientific consensus  in multiple fields to support your  theory including fields that you don't  have expertise in  and you don't have strong evidence that  other people haven't seen before  to support your theory then maybe it's  time to ask yourself if  maybe you're being a bit biased what  about the other three authors  i mentioned this before but one of the  weird things about this paper is where  it took off  it's a paper in english and the authors  are from the us and france  in france the paper took off almost  immediately after it was published it  was all over the media but in the u.s  there was barely a blip  this discrepancy is really weird  skincare people talk about sunscreen all  the time but for everyone else and the  media in general  interest in sunscreen is cyclical there  are exceptions but it's pretty rare for  people to care this much about a small  study  in a country where people mostly just  wear sunscreen to the beach if at all  so what's going on here's one suggestion  i've seen from french bloggers and  beauty experts  if you look at the conflicts of interest  section of the paper that ocean research  institute that four of the authors work  at  has partnerships with pierre fabrera a  huge french cosmetic and pharmaceutical  company  a few days after this paper was  published pierre farber announced that  they were launching a sunscreen with a  new filter called tri-sorb  and octocryllin-free features heavily in  their marketing the timing and the hype  that was almost entirely isolated to one  country  does seem a bit suspicious but pierre  farber have denied any involvement  i don't think that a conflict of  interest means that the science will  automatically be compromised  it's unfortunate that science is  expensive and a lot of topics just  aren't that high up on the priority list  for the government to fund them without  commercial interest  but it's a bit of a strange  juxtaposition with all this stuff in the  paper about marketing propaganda and big  sunscreen  another study came out from a belgium  research group at the end of march  and they found that benzophenone  increased after octocrylline sunscreens  were kept at high temperatures  so again i don't think the experiment  part of this study was wrong but if you  compare the two papers you can see how  differently the two papers contextualize  their findings  the other paper mentions that  benzophenone is a potential endocrine  disruptor and carcinogen  but it really highlights the potential  part there are a bunch of other things i  haven't mentioned that to me  also seem like pretty big red flags for  example donato's public email address  for a while was chemicals are toxic  gmail.com dancers lab runs a program  that's a lot like the ewg approved label  where they'll certify that your  sunscreens are reef safe  for a fee last minute edit last week one  of the authors from the lab  affiliated with pierre fabra commented  on my coral reef posts about adding info  on octocrylean being bad  i'm not sure why he's reaching out to  someone he called a propaganda source  seems kinda sus so what are the  takeaways from this  with octocrylene and benzophenone the  vast majority of scientists don't think  that benzophenone is as bad as the paper  makes it out to be  but the amount can get higher than  expected although it is still within the  margin of safety for oral exposure  so hopefully there'll be a bit more  research on how benzophenone affects  humans when we put it on our skin  and sunscreen formulators will focus on  ways of stabilizing octocrylene  in the meantime as many skincare experts  have said  it's probably still safe to use  octocrylene sunscreens as long as you're  not allergic to them but it's safest to  not use sunscreens after they're used by  date  and not expose them to high temperatures  which you were hopefully doing anyway  the wider takeaway is that you can't  trust everything you read just because  it's in a peer-reviewed paper  this paper had five authors who all  apparently signed off on this  and there were four anonymous reviewers  as well stuff slips through peer review  all the time  especially when it's commentary around  the relevance of the research  that part isn't checked anywhere near as  thoroughly as the actual experimental  part of the paper  now for some propaganda from big  learning if you like poisoning your  brain with education you're going to  love this  the first thousand to click the link in  the description will get a free trial of  skillshare's premium membership  skillshare is an online community with a  massive range of classes on all sorts of  propagandary topics like photography  design video editing time management  classes are mostly less than an hour so  you can quickly level up and get those  propaganda bugs  i usually use skillshare to help me make  better content for big sunscreen  but recently i've been watching a lot of  classes about cooking there's a lot of  big delicious propaganda on there from  real life chefs  i recently took a class to sharpen ha my  knife skills with elena carp from plated  and apparently i was holding my knife  wrong the whole time and now i'm the  latest victim of big  not chopping your fingers off it's less  than 10 a month with an annual  subscription check out the link in the  description
 

